{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Midianite men, traders, passed by.</b> Past the pit into which Joseph had been cast, and they heard him crying and weeping." "<b>And they drew up.</b> The Midianites drew up Joseph and brought him up from the pit, and they sold him to the Ishmaelites, and the Ishmaelites handed him over as a deposit into the hand of the Midianites, and he was sold to Potiphar from the hand of both of them — and three verses were fulfilled: 'the Midianites sold him to Egypt, to Potiphar'; and this verse: 'Potiphar purchased him from the hand of the Ishmaelites'; and another verse: 'you sold me to Egypt' — that is, through your doing.'" "<b>From the pit.</b> And his brothers knew nothing of all this, and when Reuben went to the pit and did not find him, they all concluded: a bad beast has devoured him. And they did not lie to their father, for had they sold him to someone, he would have had the resources of a nation and a kingdom in all four directions of the world to seek him out until they knew for certain whether he was alive or dead. And furthermore, if they were in doubt about him, whether he was alive or dead, why did he not recognize him by appearance or by speech — and in 'May El grant you grace, my son,' and in the five extra portions that were his, and in that he seated them before him, the firstborn according to his birthright and the youngest according to his youth? Rather, it is as has been explained. — Another interpretation: while they were still speaking among themselves, 'Come and let us sell him to the Ishmaelites who are coming upon us,' and before the Ishmaelites had reached them — Midianite men, traders, passed by, and they sold him to them while he was still in the pit, lest he weep before them and they be put to shame by him. <b>And they drew up</b> — the Midianites — <b>and brought up Joseph from the pit</b> — for they had already purchased him from his brothers' hand. And while they were drawing him up from the pit, the Ishmaelites came upon them, and the Midianites sold Joseph to the Ishmaelites, and the Ishmaelites sold him to the Midianites, and the Midianites to Potiphar — behold, here are four sales. And as for what is written further on, 'Potiphar purchased him from the hand of the Ishmaelites' — this is how the matter unfolded: the tribes sold him to the Midianites, and this sale was not written, since it was only temporary; and the Midianites sold him to the Ishmaelites; and the Ishmaelites to the Midianites — and this third sale was not written, because the third time the Ishmaelites sold him hastily and secretly, for they said: perhaps the Midianites will reconsider buying him; and the Midianites sold him to Potiphar. When Potiphar saw Joseph, that he was beautiful of appearance, in the hand of the Midianites — for the Midianites were traders, since they are brothers to the Ishmaelites — he was astonished and said: 'A German selling a Kushite, and not a Kushite selling a German!' — meaning, a beautiful person. Potiphar said: surely this one is no slave; give me a guarantee that you did not steal him. The Midianites went and brought the Ishmaelites, and they guaranteed that they had not stolen him. And that is what is written: 'Potiphar purchased him from the hand of the Ishmaelites' — meaning, from their guarantee, as in 'I myself will guarantee him; from my hand you may seek him.'" "<b>For twenty pieces of silver.</b> And would a person as handsome as Joseph be sold for such a paltry sum? Rather, because his face turned greenish in the pit on account of the snakes, they did not sell him for more. And each of the brothers took two pieces of silver as his share, to buy bread and shoes, as is said of their selling: 'they sell the righteous for silver, and the needy for a pair of shoes.' — Another interpretation: <b>For twenty pieces of silver</b> — for thus this valuation is fixed in the section, as it is written: 'and if from five years old to twenty years old, then your valuation of the male shall be twenty shekels.' And Joseph had not reached the category of twenty at that point, as it is written: 'Joseph was seventeen years old when he shepherded his brothers' flocks.' HaShem said: you sold the firstborn son of Rachel for five selas — therefore each and every one of you shall set aside five selas of firstborn-money, in the Tyrian standard. Rabbi Yehuda bar Simon said: HaShem said to the tribes — you sold the son of Rachel for five selas; therefore each and every one of you shall give a half-shekel per head."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {2 "The word גרמני and its pairing with כושי is an idiom; rendered as in the original, matching the contrast of fair-skinned (German) vs. dark-skinned (Kushite). The phrase 'this is how the matter unfolded' renders כך היה המעשה. The multiple names for the brothers — 'the tribes' (השבטים) and 'his brothers' (אחיו) — are kept as Chizkuni wrote them, reflecting different stages of the narrative."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:57.261268618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}